A resource path produces a `Path` value. `Path` is a compile-time type: the compiler uses it while building, and it never exists while your program runs.

### Using a Path

Put a `Path` straight into a template.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    logo #Path = @assets/logo.svg

    image #= [$html:
        <img src="[logo]" alt="Moth">
    ]
]

`image` is an ordinary `String`. You can compose it, store it in a constant and pass it around like any other string.

### The compiler writes the URL

You never write the output path yourself. The builder decides where the file is emitted, then writes the link from the page that contains it.

The same logo becomes `./assets/logo.svg` on the home page and `../../assets/logo.svg` on a page two folders deep.

### Compile-time only

A `Path` cannot be stored at runtime, so these are rejected:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    logo ~Path = @assets/logo.svg

    show |image Path| -> String:
        return ""
    ;
]

A mutable binding and a function parameter both need to hold the value at runtime. If you need link text at runtime, insert the `Path` into a template and use the resulting `String`.
